<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humayun Shah Bahmani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1458">1458</a>–<a href="/years/1461">1461</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Humayun Shah was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> from 1458 to 1461, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-ahmad-shah">Ala-ud-Din Ahmad Shah</a>. He appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> as a leading minister, but his short reign was remembered for harsh punishment of rebels and rivals, which earned him the name &quot;the Cruel&quot; in later chronicles. He was succeeded by his young son Nizam Shah.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alauddin Humayun Shah Bahmani was the eldest son of Alau'd-din Ahmad Shah. When his father died, a group of nobles that included Saif Khan, Mallu Khan and Shah Habibullah raised his younger brother Hasan Khan to the throne instead. Humayun, with a personal guard of eighty horsemen, seized it for himself. Saif Khan was executed, Mallu Khan fled to the Carnatic, and Hasan Khan and Shah Habibullah were imprisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his accession Humayun made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> lieutenant of the kingdom and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, with the title Malik-ut-Tujjar. His cousin Sikandar Khan, given charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, was dissatisfied and rebelled with the support of his father Jalal Khan and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velama">Velama</a> chiefs, marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. Sikandar refused the peace Humayun offered and was killed in the battle that followed. While the sultan was in Telangana, Hasan Khan escaped from prison by fraud and proclaimed himself king at Bir, but he was defeated, fled towards Vijayanagara, and was taken by Siraj Khan Junaidi and sent back to the capital.</p><p>In 1460 Hasan and his followers were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, and the punishments for which Humayun became known followed: Hasan was thrown to a tiger, some of his companions were cast into boiling cauldrons, and elephants and other wild animals were set upon others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Humayun died on 1 September 1461, either naturally or killed in his sleep by a maidservant. His wife Nargis Begum, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Taj ud-Din Firuz Shah</a>, became regent for their son; two sons became the twelfth and thirteenth sultans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> calls him Zalim, the Cruel, but modern scholars such as Haroon Khan Sherwani and Ghulam Yazdani hold that his cruelty has been exaggerated, Sherwani calling him a ruler of the &quot;ordinary Bahmani type&quot; and a strict disciplinarian. Legend says his tomb in the Bahmani tombs complex split when his body was laid in it; the damage came from a 19th-century lightning strike.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Humayun Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
